# walls — the outer walls of the main block: rusticated ground storey, string course, ashlar,
#         and quoins at every external corner.
#
# The plain rectangle of the main block, x -16.00 .. 16.00 by z -7.50 .. 7.50, 0.90 thick (D),
# standing on the podium at 1.80 and running up to the main cornice line at 11.90. It does not
# cut a single opening: every window and every door is a void owned by the fragment that draws
# it, taken away by the manifest after everything is assembled.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD: the largest flat surface in the building, and therefore the
# no-two-voxels-alike variation, which up here has to carry the whole look on its own.
#
# Above the string course this wall is a PLANE. 5.85 to 9.90 of it, all the way round a 94 m
# perimeter — about 380 m² outside and as much again inside — with no moulding, no relief, no
# shadow and nothing at all for the eye to hold on to except the surface itself. Everywhere else
# in this building the geometry does the work. Here there is no geometry to do it, and if this
# wall comes back as a slab of flat grey then the variation, the paint stack and the material
# table are not doing theirs.
#
# So the surface is built to be looked at at three scales at once, all of them SURFACE and none
# of them geometry:
#
#   metres        stone from three beds — limestone, with patches of a paler and a warmer stone
#                 keyed on one broad fbm, so the wall is not one colour anywhere
#   0.03 m        the ashlar joints, PAINTED and not cut: a 0.03 m line (M/15, the same width as
#                 the fillet between two flutes on a column) that is ONE VOXEL at the contract's
#                 metre 32. A one-voxel dark line on a flat pale field, with no geometry under it
#                 to help a denoiser guess where it goes, is the hardest thing on this wall
#   one voxel     the manifest's `variation colour=0.030 rough=0.070`, which is the whole point:
#                 five base materials on this part means five base records for it to perturb, and
#                 the measured answer is the report line `variation ... distinct records`
#
# MEASURED at the contract's metre 32, on requests/walls-probe.clip, which is this part with the
# other twenty fragments' paint rules taken off it: 27,359,448 voxels, 834.94 m³, 2234.50 m² of
# surface, ONE component and nothing floating. The two figures before the assembly order was fixed
# — see THE PART, below — were 27,340,500 voxels and 2244.95 m², so the wall gained 0.58 m³ of the
# quoin the rustication cutter had been shaving off it and lost 10.5 m² of the surface that was
# wrapped round the one-voxel fins left behind.
#
# `--clip-part part_walls` on the manifest at metre 32 reported 1,000,008 distinct visual records,
# largest identical group 588. That number is from before this change and is still the number: the
# paint stack at the bottom of this file has not been touched. On a path trace the
# plane reads as granular stone and not as flat grey, which was the question asked. Two caveats,
# both written up with their arithmetic in clips/facility/requests/walls.md: that million is the
# whole clip's default variation budget, spent by this one fragment, and it is also very nearly
# the arithmetic ceiling — colour=0.030 can only reach sixteen values of an eight-bit channel, so
# ONE base material can never produce more than 16*16*16*36 = 147,456 distinct records however
# many voxels it covers. The four extra materials this file paints are most of why the number is
# a million and not two hundred thousand.
#
# DO NOT CUT THE JOINTS INTO THE STONE. The moment they are geometry this stops being a test of
# the surface path and becomes another relief wall like everything else in the building. They are
# paint on a plane on purpose. For the same reason the ashlar storey has no rustication, no
# panels and no band courses: the flatness is the load.
#
# The rusticated storey below is the other half of the bargain — 0.09 wide by 0.135 deep channels
# on a 0.60 course, over four hundred of them round the building, which is where the sampler has
# to settle a great many small boxes cut out of one very large one.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45. Every number below is one of those or a simple fraction of one.
# The ones that look odd, and why:
#
#   16.00, 7.50   the contract's own block outline. They are not module multiples; the contract
#                 says so and everything else here is measured from them.
#   0.60          the rustication course. The contract fixes it (`courses`, `ashlar`) and the
#                 brief names it. It is 4M/3.
#   0.675, 1.35   the ashlar course and block, 1.5 M and 3 M. Chosen so that SIX courses fit
#                 exactly between the string course at 5.85 and the column head at 9.90, and so
#                 that two blocks make one bay of 2.70.
#   1.75          1.80 less the 0.05 the brief requires every part to bury into what it stands on.
#                 An overlap, not a dimension.
#   0.09, 0.135   0.2 M and 0.3 M — the width and the depth of a channel, and the same two numbers
#                 again for the joint between two quoin stones and how far a quoin stands proud.
#   0.51          0.60 less that 0.09 joint: the height of one quoin stone on a 0.60 course.
#   0.03          M/15, the painted mortar joint. The same number as the flat fillet between two
#                 flutes on a column, and for the same reason: one voxel at metre 32.
#   0.10, 0.20    not dimensions and never seen: the slack on a cutter that has to reach past the
#                 face it cuts, and the margin on a paint zone that has to reach past the surface
#                 it paints. Anything the eye can measure is above; these are tolerances.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE THICKNESS. 0.90, EVERYWHERE, AND IT IS A PROMISE TO THE REST OF THE BUILDING
# ================================================================================================
#
# This is the number four other fragments measure their openings and their rooms from, and until
# now none of them could read it anywhere: it was implied by two boxes forty lines below and
# nowhere stated. It is stated here, as three dials, in the file that builds the wall.
#
#   wall_thick   0.90 = D = 2 M    the outer wall, at every height and on all four faces
#   wall_cut     1.00              how deep a cutter must reach from the outer face to go through
#   wall_relief  0.18              the most anything on this wall stands proud of that face
#
# THE FACES. Outer on the contract's own block outline — x = +-16.00, z = +-7.50, both storeys,
# with no set-back at the string course. Inner on x = +-15.10 and z = +-6.60, which is that outline
# less D and is a number seven other fragments already write down:
#
#   entablature.clip  "the wall below is 0.90 thick with its inner face on +-15.10 and +-6.60"
#   ballroom.clip     "x = 15.10 (block_x 16.00 less D)", "z = -6.60 (block_z 7.50 less a 2 M wall)"
#   chapel, enfilade, library, salon, stair    all stand their rooms against +-15.10 / +-6.60
#
# So the thickness is not free to change. It is the inside face of seven other files, and moving it
# by a module would move every state room in the building. It stays at D, and what this file owes
# everybody instead is to SAY so, and to be that thick everywhere anybody measures it. Measured, at
# the contract's metre 32, by `--slice y@h` on requests/walls-corner.clip:
#
#   plain, ashlar storey and rusticated alike    0.906   0.90 to the nearest voxel — 29 of them
#   in a rustication channel, horizontal or vertical    0.781   0.90 less the channel's 0.135
#   at a quoin                                  1.031   0.90 and the 0.135 it stands proud
#   behind the string course band               1.094   0.90 and the corona's 0.18 of projection
#
# Those are the OUTER face moving. The inner face is +-15.10 and +-6.60 at every height on all four
# faces and nothing this file draws touches it: `--gap z@0.30,8.00` on the whole ring reads 13.250 m
# of clear air between the two long walls and `--gap x@8.00,0.30` reads 30.250 m between the ends,
# at every height either was asked at.
#
# WHAT MAY BE CUT THROUGH IT, WHICH IS THE PART THAT HAS BEEN GETTING THIS WRONG. A window or a
# door that starts on the outer face and wants to come out the other side needs to reach 0.90, and
# 1.00 with this file's own cutter slack of 0.10 on the end of it — the same slack the rustication
# channels use to reach past the face they cut. It does NOT need 1.80.
#
# 1.80 is what windows.clip and doors.clip both cut, and 0.90 of that is hole hanging in the room
# beyond the wall. That is not an abstraction. halls.clip stops its two great halls at x = 13.95
# instead of 15.10 and says why in its own header — "0.90 PAST the inner face of a 0.90 wall" —
# leaving a 1.15 m strip of nothing along each end of the building. chapel.clip carries "the strip
# z -6.65 .. -5.70 along my whole south side is a hole I do not [want]" and builds its own window
# embrasures because the cutter that should have made them stops 0.90 too far in. ballroom.clip
# records the same 0.90 in capitals. Four fragments deformed around one number, and the number was
# only ever a guess at what this wall might turn out to be.
#
# It is 0.90. It was always 0.90. Written up for them in requests/walls-thickness.md.
param wall_thick   0.90
param wall_cut     1.00
param wall_relief  0.18

# ================================================================================================
# THE RING — 0.90 thick, 1.75 to 11.90
# ================================================================================================
#
# Outer face on the contract's block outline, ±16.00 and ±7.50, FOR BOTH STOREYS. The rusticated
# storey is not set forward and the ashlar is not set back: the rustication reads by what is cut
# out of it, not by where its face is. That is deliberate and it is for the neighbours — the
# entablature, the pilasters and the window surrounds all take ±16.00 / ±7.50 from the contract
# and will land flush on this wall without having read this file.
#
# The core is cut through in y beyond both ends so the ring is open top and bottom; the roof and
# the podium close it.
let walls_outer = box -16.00 1.75 -7.50   16.00 11.90 7.50 round=0.015
let walls_core  = box -15.10 1.55 -6.60   15.10 12.10 6.60 round=0.015
let walls_ring  = difference { walls_outer walls_core }

# ================================================================================================
# THE RUSTICATED GROUND STOREY — 1.80 to 5.40, six courses of 0.60
# ================================================================================================
#
# Channels 0.09 across (0.2 M) and 0.135 deep (0.3 M), cut out of the face rather than left
# between applied blocks, which is what a channelled rustication is. Five horizontal joints at
# 2.40, 3.00, 3.60, 4.20 and 4.80, and vertical joints every 1.35 (3 M) staggered half a block
# course by course, so it is a running bond and not a grid.
#
# The horizontal channel is one FRAME — the difference of two boxes — so it wraps all four faces
# and both corners in one shape and the mitre is free. It is repeated in y; the repeat's child
# has to sit in the first cell, at the origin, and the whole repeat is then carried up to where
# the joints belong, which is why the translate is on the outside.

let walls_hchan_out = box -16.10 -0.045 -7.60    16.10 0.045 7.60
let walls_hchan_in  = box -15.865 -0.20 -7.365   15.865 0.20 7.365
let walls_hchan_one = difference { walls_hchan_out walls_hchan_in }
let walls_hchan     = translate { repeat { walls_hchan_one } y=0.60 ny=2 } 0 3.60 0

# The vertical joints. One bar, one course tall and one channel wide, cutting into one face; it is
# repeated across the face and up it, and the two sets — even courses and odd courses — differ
# only by half a block in x and half the repeat period in y. Built once on the north face and once
# on the east face and folded to the other two, which is also what keeps it symmetric about x = 0.
#
# BUILT ON THE NORTH FACE AND THE EAST FACE, WHICH IS TO SAY ON POSITIVE z AND POSITIVE x, AND
# THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE. `mirror` folds the coordinate to its ABSOLUTE VALUE, so what it shows
# on both sides is whatever the child has on the POSITIVE side; a child that lives entirely on
# negative z is asked about at +|z|, finds nothing there, and disappears from the building
# altogether. It does not warn, it does not halve, it deletes. This file was written the first
# time with the joints, the string course and every quoin on the south and east faces, and the
# result parsed, measured, reported one component and rendered — with no quoins anywhere, no
# vertical rustication on the north or south wall, and a string course that only existed on the
# two ends. `--clip-part walls_quoins` reported zero components, which is how it was found.
# South-facing work goes on the north face and gets folded. Always.
let walls_vbar_n = box -0.045 -0.30 7.365    0.045 0.30 7.60  round=0.012
let walls_vbar_e = box 15.865 -0.30 -0.045   16.10 0.30 0.045 round=0.012

let walls_vjoint_n_even = translate { repeat { walls_vbar_n } x=1.35 y=1.20 nx=13 ny=1 } 0     3.30 0
let walls_vjoint_n_odd  = translate { repeat { walls_vbar_n } x=1.35 y=1.20 nx=13 ny=1 } 0.675 3.90 0
let walls_vjoint_e_even = translate { repeat { walls_vbar_e } z=1.35 y=1.20 nz=7 ny=1 } 0 3.30 0
let walls_vjoint_e_odd  = translate { repeat { walls_vbar_e } z=1.35 y=1.20 nz=7 ny=1 } 0 3.90 0.675

let walls_vjoint_n = mirror { union { walls_vjoint_n_even walls_vjoint_n_odd } } axis=z
let walls_vjoint_e = mirror { union { walls_vjoint_e_even walls_vjoint_e_odd } } axis=x

# AND THE WHOLE CUTTER STOPS AT 5.31, WHICH IS THE UNDERSIDE OF THE STRING COURSE.
#
# The top course of the rustication runs 4.80 to 5.40 and the odd vertical bar is one course tall,
# so it ended at 5.40 — and the astragal that starts the string course begins at 5.31 and is only
# buried 0.045 into a face this bar cuts 0.135 out of. Untrimmed, every odd joint on the building
# ran up BEHIND that moulding: the neck was severed once a bay, all the way round, and what was
# left of it was roofing a 0.09 pocket with nothing behind it. Measured at `--slice y@5.35`, 0.781 m
# of wall at each joint where the run reads 1.000 everywhere else — and the string course's own
# note, "there is no hairline anywhere for the manifest's grain to open", was false at twenty-four
# places on every face.
#
# 5.31 is the astragal's foot, so the joint now runs the full height of the face anybody can see
# and stops exactly where the moulding covers it. The box is generous on the other five sides
# (+-16.20, +-7.70, 1.70) because it is trimming a CUTTER: it must not clip the 0.10 of slack the
# channels need to reach past the face they cut.
let walls_channels = intersection {
    union { walls_hchan walls_vjoint_n walls_vjoint_e }
    box -16.20 1.70 -7.70   16.20 5.31 7.70
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE QUOINS — every external corner, 1.80 to 9.90
# ================================================================================================
#
# Long and short alternating course by course, 0.90 on one face and 0.45 on the other and then
# the other way about, standing 0.135 proud (0.3 M) and buried 0.135 into the wall so nothing can
# come adrift. Each block is 0.51 tall on a 0.60 pitch, which leaves a 0.09 joint between two of
# them — the only line on the ashlar storey that is cut rather than painted.
#
# 0.135 by 0.09 is the rusticated storey's own channel, depth and width, carried up the corner:
# the quoins are that masonry continued, which is what quoins over a rusticated base are for.
# It took two goes to get there. At 0.045 proud with a 0.045 joint they did not exist at metre 16
# — one voxel of projection is a paint job, not a quoin — and at 0.09 with a 0.045 joint the
# strip was visible and the individual stones in it were not. What makes a quoin read is the
# joint between two of them, not how far it stands out; the projection only has to be enough to
# put the joint in shadow.
#
# Built at the NORTH-EAST corner — positive x, positive z — and folded twice. See the note on the
# vertical joints above for why it has to be that corner and not any of the other three. The two
# courses are two shapes, each repeated every 1.20 so that together they alternate every 0.60, and
# the whole set is trimmed at 9.90 — the column head, where the entablature takes over and a
# projecting quoin would be buried in it.

let walls_qa_x = box 15.865 0 6.735   16.135 0.51 7.635 round=0.012
let walls_qa_z = box 15.685 0 7.365   16.135 0.51 7.635 round=0.012
let walls_qb_x = box 15.865 0 7.185   16.135 0.51 7.635 round=0.012
let walls_qb_z = box 15.235 0 7.365   16.135 0.51 7.635 round=0.012

let walls_quoin_a = union { walls_qa_x walls_qa_z }
let walls_quoin_b = union { walls_qb_x walls_qb_z }

let walls_quoin_a_all = translate { repeat { walls_quoin_a } y=1.20 ny=7 } 0 1.80 0
let walls_quoin_b_all = translate { repeat { walls_quoin_b } y=1.20 ny=7 } 0 2.40 0
let walls_quoin_stack = intersection {
    union { walls_quoin_a_all walls_quoin_b_all }
    box 15.00 1.80 6.00   16.30 9.90 7.80
}
let walls_quoins = mirror { mirror { walls_quoin_stack } axis=x } axis=z

# ================================================================================================
# THE STRING COURSE — 5.40 to 5.85 (12 M to 13 M), projecting 0.18 (0.4 M)
# ================================================================================================
#
# The line the whole elevation is divided on: the rustication stops under it and the piano
# nobile's plain ashlar starts above it. Four members, drawn as sections and run straight:
#
#   5.310 - 5.400   astragal   0.09   the necking, springing out of the rusticated face
#   5.400 - 5.5875  corona     0.1875 a plain bold band with a flat soffit — the shadow line
#   5.5875- 5.6250  fillet     0.0375 set back 0.045, so the corona has an edge
#   5.6250- 5.8500  ovolo      0.225  the weathering, doming back into the ashlar face
#
# A moulding run is only the band between its two corners, so every one of them starts INSIDE the
# wall — 0.045 for the two curves, 0.18 for the two square members — and there is no hairline
# anywhere for the manifest's grain to open.
#
# It turns the corner by union and nothing else. Two perpendicular runs, each carried out to the
# other's outermost projection, meet in exactly the mitre a mason would cut: at any height the
# north run says "solid within f(y) of z = 7.50" and the east run says "solid within f(y) of
# x = 16.00", and their union is the square corner block whose two faces trace the profile both
# ways. That is why the north run reaches x = ±16.18 and the east run z = ±7.68.
#
# Drawn on the NORTH face and folded, for the reason given under the vertical joints: a run built
# on the south face is a run that is not in the building.

let walls_sc_neck_n  = astragal -16.18 5.3100 7.455   16.18 5.4000 7.590
let walls_sc_band_n  = fillet   -16.18 5.4000 7.320   16.18 5.5875 7.680
let walls_sc_step_n  = fillet   -16.18 5.5875 7.320   16.18 5.6250 7.635
let walls_sc_slope_n = ovolo    -16.18 5.6250 7.455   16.18 5.8500 7.635

let walls_sc_neck_e  = astragal 15.955 5.3100 -7.68   16.090 5.4000 7.68
let walls_sc_band_e  = fillet   15.820 5.4000 -7.68   16.180 5.5875 7.68
let walls_sc_step_e  = fillet   15.820 5.5875 -7.68   16.135 5.6250 7.68
let walls_sc_slope_e = ovolo    15.955 5.6250 -7.68   16.135 5.8500 7.68

let walls_sc_north = union { walls_sc_neck_n walls_sc_band_n walls_sc_step_n walls_sc_slope_n }
let walls_sc_east  = union { walls_sc_neck_e walls_sc_band_e walls_sc_step_e walls_sc_slope_e }
let walls_string   = union { mirror { walls_sc_north } axis=z  mirror { walls_sc_east } axis=x }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART — AND THE CHANNELS CUT THE FIELD, NOT THE DRESSINGS
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE ORDER OF THESE TWO LINES IS THE WHOLE OF IT, and it was the other way round until it was
# measured. `difference { union { ring quoins string } channels }` runs the rustication cutter over
# the quoins as well as over the wall, and a cutter sized for one face does not fit another:
#
#   the cutter reaches 0.10 past the face it cuts — to x 16.10, z 7.60 — which is the right slack
#   for a face at 16.00 and 7.50, and is exactly what the header calls a tolerance.
#   a quoin stands 0.135 PROUD of that face, to x 16.135 and z 7.635.
#
# 0.135 less 0.10 is 0.035, and that is what was left standing. At every one of the five horizontal
# channels, and at every vertical joint that fell on a quoin, a 0.035 m fin of quoin survived a cut
# it should have been immune to. **0.035 m is 1.1 voxels at the contract's metre 32** — the
# thinnest thing in the building, on the corner of it, and nobody drew it. Measured before the
# change, `--slice y@2.42` on requests/walls-corner.clip at the north-east corner:
#
#   x 16.094 .. 16.125   0.031 m   a fin standing across the channel, one voxel thick
#   z 7.594 .. 7.625     0.031 m   the same fin on the other face of the same corner
#
# and at metre 8, where a 0.035 fin cannot be resolved at all, it did not disappear quietly: the
# corner crumbled into pieces touching nothing. `part_walls` reported **21 components and 132
# floating voxels**, 33 at each of the four corners, every one of them a piece of quoin cut off by
# a channel. It is 1 component and 0 floating now, and the whole facility went from 804 components
# and 6227 floating voxels to 776 and 6111.
#
# So: the channels are cut out of the FIELD of the wall, which is what a rusticated field is, and
# the quoins and the string course are laid on afterwards, which is what a dressing is. That is
# also the masonry — the channels now run out of the wall and stop dead against the quoin.

let walls_field = difference { walls_ring walls_channels }
let part_walls  = union { walls_field walls_quoins walls_string }

# ================================================================================================
# THE SURFACE — where the load actually lives
# ================================================================================================
#
# Zones first, all of them shapes and all of them mine. A paint rule keyed on a shape is settled
# for a whole block at once by the sampler; one keyed on a pattern has to be asked per voxel,
# everywhere in the clip, so the pattern rules below are kept to four and all four hang off
# `walls_ring`, which is two boxes, rather than off the assembled part, which is a hundred.
#
# Measured at metre 16, by commenting rules out one group at a time:
#   the three shape rules   2,004,676 field evaluations   0.6 per solid voxel — they settle
#   the four pattern rules 13,161,088 field evaluations   4.0 per solid voxel — one each, per voxel
# and the four pattern rules are what take the surface from 671,352 distinct records to 787,779.
# That is the trade, stated so the next person can decide it again rather than discover it.

let walls_lining = intersection { part_walls  box -15.30 1.55 -6.80   15.30 12.10 6.80 }
let walls_rustic = intersection { part_walls  box -16.40 1.55 -7.90   16.40 5.40 7.90 }

let walls_band_ash = intersection { walls_ring  box -16.30 5.85 -7.80   16.30 11.90 7.80 }
let walls_ash_ns   = intersection { walls_band_ash  mirror { box -16.30 5.85 6.55   16.30 11.90 7.80 } axis=z }
let walls_ash_ew   = intersection { walls_band_ash  mirror { box 15.05 5.85 -7.80   16.30 11.90 7.80 } axis=x }

# The bond. `bricks` lays its courses along y and its length along x only when it faces z, so the
# east and west walls get the same pattern turned a quarter turn about y — the courses stay level
# and the length runs along z, which is the only way to get a running bond onto a wall whose
# normal is x. The lift of 0.45 puts a bed joint exactly on 5.85, and then the six courses of
# 0.675 land on 6.525, 7.20, 7.875, 8.55, 9.225 and 9.90: the string course and the column head.
#
# mortar=0.03 is M/15. It is what makes the painted joint one voxel wide at metre 32, and it is
# the number this part is being judged on. Do not widen it to make the wall look tidier.
let walls_bond_z = translate { bricks length=1.35 height=0.675 mortar=0.03 facing=z } 0 0.45 0
let walls_bond_x = rotate { walls_bond_z } y=0.25

# `bricks` is positive in the joint and negative on a stone face, so `0.02 - it` crosses the
# rules' own 0.02 exactly where the joint begins. Maxed against the zone, which is how a pattern
# is confined to one part's own geometry: outside the zone the shape's distance is what the rule
# reads, and it is far too big to pass.
let walls_joint_ns = max { walls_ash_ns  add { constant 0.02  negate { walls_bond_z } } }
let walls_joint_ew = max { walls_ash_ew  add { constant 0.02  negate { walls_bond_x } } }

# Three beds of stone in one wall. One broad grain, read high for the paler stone and low for the
# warmer one, which leaves the limestone in between — so no two square metres of this face are the
# same colour and none of it is a pattern anybody can name.
#
# The three are DELIBERATELY CLOSE: plaster 226,222,212, limestone 208,203,190, stucco 214,206,188,
# and all three matte within thirty of each other in roughness. The first pass used marble_hot and
# sandstone, which are 232 and 196 — thirty-six apart — and the wall came back looking like
# camouflage: big warm blobs on a pale field, a wall that had been *decorated* to hide the fact
# that nothing else was happening on it. That is the failure this part is supposed to avoid from
# the other side. Three stones a few values apart is what an ashlar face actually is, and it
# leaves the per-voxel variation, not the paint, as the thing carrying the surface.
#
# Five octaves over a bay, not three over a metre and a half: large, fractal and slow-drifting
# reads as stone out of different beds, where small and round reads as damp.
let walls_bed      = fbm size=2.70 octaves=5 seed=71
let walls_bed_pale = max { walls_band_ash  add { constant 0.30  negate { walls_bed } } }
let walls_bed_warm = max { walls_band_ash  add { constant 0.30  walls_bed } }

# The coats, in order: later covers earlier.
paint limestone  where=part_walls        below=0.02
paint sandstone  where=walls_rustic      below=0.02
paint plaster    where=walls_bed_pale    below=0.02
paint stucco     where=walls_bed_warm    below=0.02
